Start by checking whether Heat Map is enabled in your target container by running the following command:

SQL> show parameter HEAT_MAP


Enable Heat Map either across all containers (CDB$ROOT) or only for a specific PDB:

-- Enable Heat Map in all PDBs (run in CDB$ROOT)
ALTER SYSTEM SET heat_map = ON CONTAINER=ALL;

-- Enable Heat Map only in one PDB
ALTER SESSION SET CONTAINER = PDB;
ALTER SYSTEM SET heat_map = ON;


For the tiering demo, we need an “archive” tablespace. If Oracle-Managed Files is used, the command works without file paths as follows:

CREATE TABLESPACE tbs_archive;

In the same PDB, create a sample user with the necessary permissions:

CREATE USER demo IDENTIFIED BY demo QUOTA UNLIMITED ON users;
ALTER USER demo QUOTA UNLIMITED ON tbs_archive;
GRANT connect, resource, execute any procedure TO demo;
GRANT execute ON dbms_ilm_admin TO demo;

Connect with the newly created demo user and create a range-interval partitioned table:

sqlplus demo/demo@localhost:1521/PDB

CREATE TABLE sales_ado
(
  sale_id     NUMBER        NOT NULL,
  sale_date   DATE          NOT NULL,
  customer_id NUMBER        NOT NULL,
  amount      NUMBER(10,2)  NOT NULL,
  CONSTRAINT sales_ado_pk PRIMARY KEY (sale_id)
)
PARTITION BY RANGE (sale_date)
INTERVAL (NUMTOYMINTERVAL(1,'MONTH'))
(
  PARTITION p_start VALUES LESS THAN (DATE '2025-01-01')
);

Insert “cold” rows into P_START (old dates):

INSERT INTO sales_ado
SELECT
  LEVEL,
  DATE '2024-06-01' + MOD(LEVEL, 10),
  MOD(LEVEL, 5000) + 1,
  ROUND(DBMS_RANDOM.VALUE(10, 500), 2)
FROM dual
CONNECT BY LEVEL <= 1000;

COMMIT;

Insert “hot” rows to create interval partitions (new dates):

INSERT INTO sales_ado
SELECT
  100000 + LEVEL,
  DATE '2026-01-15' + MOD(LEVEL, 10),
  MOD(LEVEL, 5000) + 1,
  ROUND(DBMS_RANDOM.VALUE(10, 500), 2)
FROM dual
CONNECT BY LEVEL <= 1000;

COMMIT;


Gather table statistics:

BEGIN
  DBMS_STATS.GATHER_TABLE_STATS(USER, 'SALES_ADO');
END;
/

Verify partitions:

col PARTITION_NAME for a15

SELECT partition_name, tablespace_name
FROM   user_tab_partitions
WHERE  table_name = 'SALES_ADO'
ORDER  BY partition_position;


Compression tiering (segment-level example):

ALTER TABLE sales_ado
  ILM ADD POLICY
    ROW STORE COMPRESS ADVANCED
    SEGMENT AFTER 5 DAYS OF NO ACCESS;


Storage tiering (move to another tablespace):

ALTER TABLE sales_ado
  MODIFY PARTITION p_start
  ILM ADD POLICY
    TIER TO tbs_archive READ ONLY
    SEGMENT AFTER 15 DAYS OF NO ACCESS;


To do that, we run ILM manually using DBMS_ILM.EXECUTE_ILM:

SET SERVEROUTPUT ON
DECLARE
  v_task_id NUMBER;
BEGIN
  DBMS_ILM.EXECUTE_ILM(
    owner          => 'DEMO',      -- use the actual table owner
    object_name    => 'SALES_ADO', -- use the actual table name
    task_id        => v_task_id,
    execution_mode => DBMS_ILM.ILM_EXECUTION_OFFLINE
  );
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ADO task id = ' || v_task_id);
END;
/

After executing ILM, you can check whether a task was created:
SELECT task_id, state
       ,creation_time
FROM  user_ilmtasks
ORDER BY creation_time DESC;


To switch ILM policy time interpretation from days to seconds, run the following:
BEGIN
  DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.CUSTOMIZE_ILM(
    parameter => DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.POLICY_TIME,
    value     => DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.ILM_POLICY_IN_SECONDS
  );
END;
/

Don’t leave it enabled in real environments. To revert that setting, set the POLICY_TIME parameter back to the default days unit:

BEGIN
  DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.CUSTOMIZE_ILM(
    parameter => DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.POLICY_TIME,
    value     => DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.ILM_POLICY_IN_DAYS
  );
END;
/


Run the following query once, then do not touch the data:

SELECT /*+ FULL(s) NO_INDEX(s) */ SUM(s.amount)
FROM   demo.sales_ado s;


--

set linesize 800
col OBJ_NAME for a15
col SUB_NAME for a15
alter session set NLS_DATE_FORMAT='DD-MON-YYYY HH24:MI:SS';

SELECT object_name as obj_name
       ,subobject_name as sub_name
       ,track_time
       ,n_full_scan
       ,n_lookup_scan
       ,n_segment_write 
FROM v$heat_map_segment
WHERE object_name LIKE 'SALES_ADO%';

This query is showing Heat Map activity flags per segment and partition for objects whose name starts with SALES_ADO:

Here’s a quick way to see that behavior, in one session, run the following query several times:

SELECT /*+ FULL(s) NO_INDEX(s) */ SUM(s.amount)
FROM   demo.sales_ado s;


In another session, check the counters in v$heat_map_segment:

set linesize 800
col OBJ_NAME for a15
col SUB_NAME for a15
alter session set NLS_DATE_FORMAT='DD-MON-YYYY HH24:MI:SS';

SELECT object_name as obj_name
       ,subobject_name as sub_name
       ,track_time
       ,n_full_scan
       ,n_lookup_scan
       ,n_segment_write 
FROM v$heat_map_segment
WHERE object_name LIKE 'SALES_ADO%';


Now disconnect from the 1st session where you ran the full-scan query, and check the counter from the 2nd session again:

set linesize 800
col OBJ_NAME for a15
col SUB_NAME for a15
alter session set NLS_DATE_FORMAT='DD-MON-YYYY HH24:MI:SS';

SELECT object_name as obj_name
       ,subobject_name as sub_name
       ,track_time
       ,n_full_scan
       ,n_lookup_scan
       ,n_segment_write 
FROM v$heat_map_segment
WHERE object_name LIKE 'SALES_ADO%';


In the previous section, we touched on the data. Now let’s wait ~6 seconds , then force ILM execution:

SET SERVEROUTPUT ON
DECLARE
  t NUMBER;
BEGIN
  DBMS_ILM.EXECUTE_ILM(
    ilm_scope      => DBMS_ILM.SCOPE_SCHEMA,
    execution_mode => DBMS_ILM.ILM_EXECUTION_OFFLINE,
    task_id        => t
  );
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('TASK_ID='||t);
END;
/

Check the task creation:

SELECT task_id, state, creation_time
FROM   user_ilmtasks
ORDER  BY creation_time DESC;


Verify compression happened (but not move yet)

SELECT partition_name, tablespace_name, compression, compress_for
FROM   user_tab_partitions
WHERE  table_name = 'SALES_ADO'
ORDER  BY partition_position;


To find the reason, query user_ilmresults  for the task created by your manual DBMS_ILM.EXECUTE_ILM run:

SELECT comments
FROM   user_ilmresults
WHERE  task_id = 2;

Since the compression policy worked, let’s wait until total idle time > 15 seconds, then execute ILM again:

SET SERVEROUTPUT ON
DECLARE
  t NUMBER;
BEGIN
  DBMS_ILM.EXECUTE_ILM(
    ilm_scope      => DBMS_ILM.SCOPE_SCHEMA,
    execution_mode => DBMS_ILM.ILM_EXECUTION_OFFLINE,
    task_id        => t
  );
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('TASK_ID='||t);
END;
/

Check the task creation:

SELECT task_id, state, creation_time
FROM   user_ilmtasks
ORDER  BY creation_time DESC;


Verify that movement happened:

SELECT partition_name, tablespace_name, compression, compress_for
FROM   user_tab_partitions
WHERE  table_name = 'SALES_ADO'
ORDER  BY partition_position;


 
As we mentioned previously, don’t leave ILM policy time interpretation from days to seconds enabled in real environments. To revert that setting, set the POLICY_TIME parameter back to the default days unit:

BEGIN
  DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.CUSTOMIZE_ILM(
    parameter => DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.POLICY_TIME,
    value     => DBMS_ILM_ADMIN.ILM_POLICY_IN_DAYS
  );
END;
/





